# Machine agent framework (Codex)

The registry at `{{REPO_DIR}}/roles.config.json` is the single source of truth
for every agent profile on this machine. Each profile is installed here as an
`agent-<key>` skill.

{{ROSTER_SENTENCE}}

{{ROSTER_TABLE}}

## What Codex can and cannot do with this framework

**Can:** adopt any profile (load its `agent-<key>` skill and work inside it),
run the deterministic router, delegate with `spawn_agent`, and run a real code
review with the native `codex review` subcommand.

**Cannot: route a profile to its configured model.** Codex dispatches OpenAI
models; every profile in this registry is configured on a provider Codex cannot
reach. A profile you adopt runs on **your session's model**. Say that plainly if
asked — never report that a profile ran on its configured model.

`spawn_agent` gives a child a fresh context, not a profile. Its `model` field
takes OpenAI models only. If you delegate, paste the profile's mission and
boundaries into the child's task; nothing carries them automatically.

The `tools:` list in each profile is Claude Code's tool vocabulary. Codex does
not enforce it, so a read-only profile's boundary is a discipline here, not a
sandbox. Keep it anyway.

## Picking a profile

- **`runner`** is the everyday front door for routine work, maintenance, and triage.
- **`planner` → `builder`** is the development core for substantive work.
- **`code-reviewer`** runs before every PR. The Git Workflow Standard makes this
  mandatory on every harness. On Codex the native path is `codex review`
  (or `codex exec review`); adopt the `agent-code-reviewer` profile for its
  boundaries and output contract, and never write "reviewed" into a PR body
  unless a review actually ran.
- Direct-call-only profiles ({{DIRECT_CALL_ONLY}}) run only when the user asks
  for them by name.

